#+author: Henrik Frisk
This is a well written paper about the artist Stanley Keith Bowsza (Minóy), a musician and composer that deserves a much greater recognition and this paper is in that sense very welcome. The poetic language and the many references to the music of Minóy supports the general impression given by the author; that this is an interesting and valuable artist from whom there is much to learn. As a research output the paper would need a more clearly defined question and the method, describing Minóy as a desiring-machine, would require a critical description of Deleuze and Guattari's concept and why it is useful to ascribe this concept to Minóy's music.

Following are my comments and observations:

1. I am not sure whether this paper is to be seen as a research contribution or more of a general overview of the works of Minóy. If it is the former the paper needs a better contextualization as to which field of studies it belongs to. It also needs to better lay out the arguments for why Minóy's music is a desiring machine (the central claim given the title). This is not clear, and there are more references to mimetic relations between the music and the machine ("superimposed collage electronics that produces a machine-like intensity", line 58), which is far from how Deleuze and Guattari uses the term, than there are references to D&G's concept of the desiring machine. It is daunting to make sense of D&G's philosophy in music (Deleuze and Music edited by Buchanan and Swiboda is one example which could be a reference) so perhaps this is not what serves the paper best, in which case it would be necessary to explicitly claim that the theory is more of an inspiration, or loose reference.

   If it is rather an overview paper of Minóy's music, then the reference to D&G stands a bit as superficial.  One example is this sentance on line 63: "Many of his tape releases had only one or two compositions on them, thus allowing him the time to develop a machinic drone theme and hypnotically immerse the listener in what were usually vastly complex long works of audio art." The causality is not clear between what aspects of the tape releases lends themselves to the development of a machine drone, and the vague reference to Freud through the word 'hypnotically' suggests a reference to D&G's desiring-machine, but this suggestion is never confirmed, nor denied. As an overview the paper would benefit from raising the poetic descriptions of the music to the fore and more clearly stating that the reference to D&G is more of an influence.

2. The many descriptions of the music are, as already stated, beautifully shaped but could be complemented with non-expressive qualities, such as descriptions of similarities and dissimilarities between works, and techniques used. Towards the end of page 4 the word 'sample' is introduced. Can all off Minóy's music be said to be sample based or was this introduced at some specific point? The "representation of representation", introduced on line 134 is thrilling but there is far too little information for the reader to comprehend this.

3. The references to other composers on page 5 (line 180) is very good, but is, again, too brief to be helpful. How are Cage, Tenney, Riley and Oswald references here beyond of them being "sample-based audio artists" (which is only really true of John Oswald).

4. The reference to Groupe de Recherches Musicales is ambiguous (line 52). GRM is not indicative of a "compositional practice" in general. A reference to a specific composer would be more informative.
